##First, confirm the cluster name:
kind get clusters

## Load the image:
kind load docker-image fairy-bake:v1 --name helm-lab

## Create a Helm Chart for Your Application
cd ~/bakery-app

##Create a Helm chart:
helm create fairy-bake-chart

## Inspect the chart:
ls -R fairy-bake-chart

Helm creates a reusable chart containing templates, metadata, and default configuration values.

### Now open the chart's configuration

nano fairy-bake-chart/values.yaml

Find the relevant entries and change them to the following.

replicaCount: 2

image:
  repository: fairy-bake
  pullPolicy: IfNotPresent
  tag: "v1"

service:
  type: ClusterIP
  port: 80

autoscaling:
  enabled: false

Do not replace the entire generated file with only these lines. Preserve the remaining fields used by the chart's templates.

Save the file.

## Validate the Helm Chart
helm lint fairy-bake-chart

## Now render the chart:
helm template fairy-bake ./fairy-bake-chart

## Inspect the generated Deployment.
You should find an image reference resembling:
image: "fairy-bake:v1"
The exact formatting depends on the template.
Check the replica count:
replicas: 2
These are the values that Helm will use when rendering the Kubernetes resources.

## Deploy the Application Using Helm
Now install the chart.

helm install fairy-bake ./fairy-bake-chart
helm list

Expected Output
fairy-bake

## Check the Pods:
kubectl get pods

## Check the Deployment:
kubectl get deployments

##Check the Service:
kubectl get services

The generated Service name will typically resemble:
fairy-bake-fairy-bake-chart

##Inspect them:
kubectl describe pods

